      "id": "gemini-agent-01",
      "title": "Agent 本质：LLM + while 循环 + 工具",
      "category": "AI Agent",
      "tags": [
        "ai-agent",
        "tool-calling",
        "gemini",
        "en-expression"
      ],
      "question": "En-zh bro explain how ai agents work.how to write the tool parser what actly does the llm tool call api look like",
      "answer": "At its core, an AI agent is just an LLM trapped in a while loop with access to external tools. Unlike a standard chatbot that generates text once and stops, an agent follows a Reason-Act-Observe cycle: you send a prompt plus a menu of available functions, the LLM decides whether to answer directly or ask for a tool, your application code actually executes that tool locally or via API, and then you feed the result back into the LLM until it has enough context to give a final answer.\n\nCrucially, the LLM never executes code itself—it is strictly a text-in, text-out prediction engine. When an LLM \"calls a tool,\" it is simply outputting a structured payload (usually JSON or special XML tokens) that says, \"Hey, please run get_weather with city=\"Ningbo\" for me and tell me what happened.\"\n\n## 1. What the LLM Tool Call API Actually Looks Like\n\nIn modern APIs (like OpenAI's Chat Completions or Anthropic's Messages API), tool calling happens across a three-step HTTP payload exchange: sending tool definitions, receiving a tool call request, and returning the tool's output.\n\n### Step A: You send the user prompt + JSON Schema tool definitions (tools)\n\n```json\n{\n  \"model\": \"gpt-4o\",\n  \"messages\": [\n    { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What's the weather in Ningbo?\" }\n  ],\n  \"tools\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"function\",\n      \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"get_weather\",\n        \"description\": \"Get current temperature for a given city.\",\n        \"parameters\": {\n          \"type\": \"object\",\n          \"properties\": {\n            \"city\": { \"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"City name, e.g. Ningbo\" }\n          },\n          \"required\": [\"city\"]\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n### Step B: The LLM stops generating normal text and returns tool_calls\n\n```json\n{\n  \"choices\": [\n    {\n      \"finish_reason\": \"tool_calls\",\n      \"message\": {\n        \"role\": \"assistant\",\n        \"content\": null,\n        \"tool_calls\": [\n          {\n            \"id\": \"call_99x88y77z\",\n            \"type\": \"function\",\n            \"function\": {\n              \"name\": \"get_weather\",\n              \"arguments\": \"{\\\"city\\\":\\\"Ningbo\\\"}\"\n            }\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotice that `arguments` comes back as an escaped JSON string (not a parsed dictionary), and every call has a unique id so the model can match results back to requests when running parallel tool calls.\n\n### Step C: Your backend runs the function and appends a role: \"tool\" message\n\n```json\n{\n  \"model\": \"gpt-4o\",\n  \"messages\": [\n    { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What's the weather in Ningbo?\" },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"assistant\",\n      \"tool_calls\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"call_99x88y77z\",\n          \"type\": \"function\",\n          \"function\": { \"name\": \"get_weather\", \"arguments\": \"{\\\"city\\\":\\\"Ningbo\\\"}\" }\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"tool\",\n      \"tool_call_id\": \"call_99x88y77z\",\n      \"content\": \"{\\\"temp_c\\\": 18, \\\"condition\\\": \\\"Cloudy\\\"}\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## 2. How to Write a Tool Parser (and Dispatch Loop)\n\nWhen using hosted APIs (OpenAI, Gemini, Claude), the provider's inference server already parses raw model tokens into that clean tool_calls JSON object for you, so your \"parser\" is really a schema-validating dispatcher. However, if you self-host open-source models (via vLLM, llama.cpp, or raw prompts) or want bulletproof execution, your parser needs to handle two things: extracting the structured call and validating arguments safely before execution.\n\nHere is a clean, production-style Python pattern using Pydantic so malformed LLM JSON never crashes your runtime:\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport re\nfrom typing import Callable, Dict, Any\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError\n\n# 1. Define strict input schemas using Pydantic\nclass WeatherArgs(BaseModel):\n    city: str\n\ndef get_weather(city: str) -> dict:\n    return {\"city\": city, \"temp_c\": 18, \"condition\": \"Cloudy\"}\n\n# 2. Registry mapping tool names -> (PydanticValidator, ActualFunction)\nTOOL_REGISTRY: Dict[str, tuple[type[BaseModel], Callable]] = {\n    \"get_weather\": (WeatherArgs, get_weather),\n}\n\n# 3. Raw text parser (needed if your model outputs <tool_call>...</tool_call> tags)\ndef extract_raw_tool_calls(raw_text: str) -> list[dict]:\n    pattern = r\"<tool_call>\\s*(\\{.*?\\})\\s*</tool_call>\"\n    matches = re.findall(pattern, raw_text, flags=re.DOTALL)\n    parsed = []\n    for m in matches:\n        try:\n            parsed.append(json.loads(m))\n        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n            # Handle broken JSON gracefully instead of crashing\n            parsed.append({\"error\": \"Invalid JSON syntax generated by model\"})\n    return parsed\n\n# 4. Safe execution dispatcher (works for both API tool_calls & parsed raw calls)\ndef execute_tool_call(name: str, raw_arguments: str | dict) -> str:\n    if name not in TOOL_REGISTRY:\n        return json.dumps({\"error\": f\"Unknown tool '{name}'. Check available tools.\"})\n\n    schema_cls, func = TOOL_REGISTRY[name]\n\n    try:\n        args_dict = json.loads(raw_arguments) if isinstance(raw_arguments, str) else raw_arguments\n        validated_args = schema_cls(**args_dict)  # Type-checks and coerces fields\n        result = func(**validated_args.model_dump())\n        return json.dumps(result)\n    except (json.JSONDecodeError, ValidationError) as e:\n        # Feed the validation error BACK to the LLM so it can self-correct!\n        return json.dumps({\"error\": f\"Argument validation failed: {str(e)}\"})\n```\n\n## 3. Three Pro Tips That Make or Break Tool Parsers\n\n**Never raise exceptions to the user on bad tool JSON—feed errors back to the LLM.** Models are remarkably good at self-correcting when a role: \"tool\" message replies with \"Argument validation failed: 'city' is required\". Letting the agent retry once or twice turns a brittle script into an autonomous workflow.\n\n**Handle streaming chunks carefully.** When stream=True, the API does not send tool_calls in one piece; it drips arguments fragment by fragment ({\"ci, ty\":, \"Ning, bo\"}) across delta chunks indexed by tool_calls[0].index. You must buffer those string fragments per index until finish_reason == \"tool_calls\" arrives before calling json.loads().\n\n**Never use eval() or exec() to dispatch tools.** Always look up functions in an explicit allowlist dictionary (TOOL_REGISTRY). If an attacker sneaks a prompt injection into a webpage or email that your agent reads, an unrestricted dispatcher could let the LLM execute arbitrary functions.",
      "annotations": [
