    {
      "id": "gemini-agent-02",
      "title": "为什么 arguments 是 JSON 字符串而不是对象",
      "category": "AI Agent",
      "tags": [
        "ai-agent",
        "tool-calling",
        "gemini",
        "en-expression"
      ],
      "question": "Wait bro in step B llm output the full json string not a json object.why ?Because llm can only predict the next token ? but i think in my view llm dont generate the json schema when call tool. in deepseek opensource models they generate xml like text(actly not a xml but like xml syntax, they use a obscure char to pretend the xml tag \"〈\" actly not <) or maybe it is not the \"〈\" idk the unicode actually\n\numm so the api return should be transferred from llm dialect to json schema right?so why api dont return the json object but a string ?",
      "answer": "You hit the nail on the head on both counts! Your intuition about how models like DeepSeek actually emit special tokens under the hood—and how the inference server acts as a translator—is spot-on. Let's break down both parts of your question.\n\n## 1. What Open-Source Models (like DeepSeek) Actually Output Under the Hood\n\nYou are 100% right that the LLM itself does not natively output an OpenAI-style tool_calls JSON envelope. During post-training (SFT/RL), each model family is taught its own chat template dialect using dedicated special tokens (registered as single token IDs in the tokenizer vocabulary) so the model never confuses a tool call with normal user text that happens to contain < or >.\n\nFor example, DeepSeek-V3 / R1 uses full-width Unicode brackets (<｜tool▁calls▁begin｜>, <｜tool▁call▁begin｜>, <｜tool▁sep｜> — using ｜ U+FF5C and ▁ U+2581) wrapped around the function name and arguments. Qwen uses <tool_call>...</tool_call>, and Llama uses <|python_tag|>. When you run vLLM, SGLang, or DeepSeek's hosted API, their server-side tool parser intercepts those raw tokens, strips out the ｜ / ▁ dialect, and normalizes everything into the standard OpenAI API response format.\n\n## 2. So If the Server Already Translates It, Why Return arguments as a String Instead of a JSON Object?\n\nThere are three real engineering reasons why OpenAI designed arguments as a raw string instead of a parsed object—and why the entire ecosystem got locked into it:\n\n**Streaming (stream=True) would be impossible with a JSON object.** In JSON, an object ({\"city\": \"Ningbo\"}) cannot be half-valid mid-stream without constantly mutating keys or breaking schema parsers. By typing arguments as a string, the server can simply forward each token chunk (\"{\\\"ci\", \"ty\\\":\", \"\\\"Ningbo\\\"}\") as it arrives from the GPU, letting the client concatenate the string and parse it once at the end.\n\n**Fault tolerance when the LLM hallucinates broken JSON.** LLMs are probabilistic next-token predictors—sometimes they forget a closing } or mess up quotes, especially without strict constrained decoding. If the API promised arguments was a valid JSON object, a single syntax error from the LLM would force the API gateway itself to throw a 500 Internal Server Error or fail serialization. Returning a string lets the API succeed (200 OK) and hands the raw broken text to your code so you can catch JSONDecodeError and tell the LLM to fix it.\n\n**Pass-through efficiency & preserving exact formatting.** In your backend, you almost always pass arguments straight into a validator like json.loads() or Pydantic's model_validate_json(). Keeping it as a string avoids double-serialization (Server parses string → builds object → serializes HTTP JSON → Client parses HTTP JSON → re-serializes for Pydantic) and preserves exact whitespace/key ordering if you need to append the assistant's tool_calls message back into the conversation history verbatim.\n\n(Note: Newer APIs like Anthropic's Messages API and OpenAI's newer Responses API actually switched to returning input / arguments as a parsed JSON object for non-streaming calls—or sending input_json_delta events during streaming—precisely because developers kept asking this exact question!)",
      "annotations": [
