      "id": "gemini-agent-03",
      "title": "用 _explanation 字段优化工具调用等待体验",
      "category": "AI Agent",
      "tags": [
        "ai-agent",
        "tool-calling",
        "gemini",
        "en-expression"
      ],
      "question": "Bro i heard of one thing that can make the tool call wait more friendly\n\nitis justlike\n\nset a parameter for all the tool call \"explaination\"in the first parameters to ask llms to generate the sentence first (like searching for \"why xi jinpin is president\" or checking the api a11y that shit) then when the first parameter is over (not the whole toolcall object( the over signal arrives) ) then yhe backend can parse and sent to the frontend to display the tool call block cards.",
      "answer": "Yes—that is a very real, battle-tested UX trick used in production agent UIs (sometimes called an _intent or explanation field). Instead of letting the user stare at a blank screen or a generic \"Running search_web...\" spinner while the LLM slowly generates 500 tokens of complex JSON arguments (like a massive SQL query or file edit), you inject a human-readable explanation property as the very first key in every tool's JSON Schema, parse it mid-stream the instant that first field closes, and push a live status card to the frontend immediately.\n\n## 1. Why Putting explanation First Works (And One Crucial Catch)\n\nBecause autoregressive LLMs generate tokens strictly left-to-right, the order of keys in your JSON Schema properties (and required array) strongly guides the order in which the model emits keys. When explanation comes first, the model generates {\"explanation\": \"Checking API accessibility and status...\", \"url\": ...}—giving you the complete sentence within the first ~10–15 tokens, long before the heavy payload finishes streaming.\n\n**The Catch: Never execute the tool when explanation finishes—only render the UI card in a \"preparing\" / \"running\" state.** You must still wait for the full tool call (finish_reason == \"tool_calls\") before executing the backend function, because the remaining parameters are still streaming in. Also note that some strict structured-output backends sort JSON keys alphabetically, so naming it _explanation or a_explanation (or checking your provider's key-ordering behavior) ensures it stays first even if keys get sorted.\n\n## 2. Schema + Streaming Partial-Field Extractor (Python)\n\nInstead of trying to run json.loads() on broken half-JSON every chunk, you can detect the closing quote of the first string field (\"explanation\": \"...\") using a tiny regex or state check on the growing arguments buffer, emit an SSE event (tool_card_preview) to your frontend once, and let the rest of the stream finish normally.\n\n```python\nimport re\nimport json\n\n# 1. Inject `_explanation` as the FIRST property on every tool schema\ndef with_explanation(tool_schema: dict) -> dict:\n    params = tool_schema[\"function\"][\"parameters\"]\n    new_props = {\n        \"_explanation\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\",\n            \"description\": \"A short, user-friendly sentence describing what you are doing right now (e.g., 'Searching docs for API auth').\"\n        },\n        **params.get(\"properties\", {})\n    }\n    params[\"properties\"] = new_props\n    params[\"required\"] = [\"_explanation\", *params.get(\"required\", [])]\n    return tool_schema\n\n# 2. Detect when `_explanation` finishes mid-stream (handles escaped quotes \\\")\nEXPLANATION_DONE_RE = re.compile(r'^\\s*\\{\\s*\"_explanation\"\\s*:\\s*\"((?:[^\"\\\\]|\\\\.)*)\"')\n\nclass StreamingToolBuffer:\n    def __init__(self, tool_call_id: str, tool_name: str):\n        self.id = tool_call_id\n        self.name = tool_name\n        self.buffer = \"\"\n        self.card_emitted = False\n\n    def feed_delta(self, delta_chunk: str) -> dict | None:\n        self.buffer += delta_chunk\n\n        # Only check until we emit the UI card once\n        if not self.card_emitted:\n            match = EXPLANATION_DONE_RE.match(self.buffer)\n            if match:\n                self.card_emitted = True\n                # Unescape JSON string safely\n                explanation_text = json.loads(f'\"{match.group(1)}\"')\n                return {\n                    \"event\": \"tool_card_preview\",\n                    \"tool_call_id\": self.id,\n                    \"tool\": self.name,\n                    \"status\": \"preparing\",\n                    \"explanation\": explanation_text,\n                }\n        return None\n```\n\n## 3. How the End-to-End Timeline Looks to the User\n\n**Token 1–3 (tool_calls delta starts):** Your backend sees function.name = \"web_search\" and creates a StreamingToolBuffer.\n\n**Token 4–15 (\"_explanation\": \"Searching why...\" closes):** feed_delta() matches the closing quote and immediately pushes tool_card_preview over SSE. The frontend renders a sleek card saying \"Searching why...\" with a subtle pulse animation—zero awkward blank screen.\n\n**Token 16–200 (remaining args stream in):** The user comfortably reads the card while the model finishes generating query, filters, or a 200-line code patch in the background.\n\n**finish_reason == \"tool_calls\":** Backend parses the complete JSON (stripping _explanation before passing kwargs to your Python function), runs the tool, and updates the same card ID to status: \"done\" with the result.",
      "annotations": [
        {
